WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Guardrails are not a security model.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:05.848
Lesson 10’s guards reduce how often bad things happen.

3
00:00:06.128 --> 00:00:11.376
Security asks a harder question: when a guard fails, what can an attacker actually do?

4
00:00:11.656 --> 00:00:28.061
An LLM feature combines the classic web application attack surface (inputs, outputs, credentials, dependencies) with a new one: a component that follows instructions written in natural language, from anyone whose text reaches its context.

5
00:00:28.341 --> 00:00:38.133
The OWASP GenAI Security Project maintains the most widely used catalog of the resulting risks, the Top 10 for LLM Applications.

6
00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:46.456
The current edition is the 2025 list; check the site for updates, because the project revises it as attacks evolve.

7
00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:54.245
The list is published under a ShareAlike license, so this lesson summarizes it in its own words rather than adapting its text.

8
00:00:54.525 --> 00:01:02.568
Read the official entries: each one has examples, attack scenarios, and a prevention section that is more complete than any summary.

9
00:01:02.848 --> 00:01:07.499
The most useful way to learn the list is to apply it to one system you understand.

10
00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:13.837
We’ll use a realistic one: the support assistant from the lab, grown into what a team would actually ship.
